Pearl a dynamic interpreted scripting  language famous for its powerful text  processing abilities it was developed in  1987 by Larry wall a linguist who  designed it to feel more like a human  language than a computer language when  someone says Pearl they're usually  talking about version 5. there was a  version 6 under development but it  evolved into another language called  Raku Pearl is often referred to as a  glue language and has been dubbed the  Swiss Army chainsaw of the Internet it's  most commonly used to write CGI scripts  that allow web servers to communicate  with external programs its powerful  string parsing abilities has made it the  language of choice on high traffic sites  like DuckDuckGo booking.com and  Craigslist syntactically it resembles C  but is far more compressed allowing  developers to write very tourist code  like one-liners that hack together a  solution much quicker than other  scripting languages like python it  contains a large collection of language  Primitives and a ton of shortcuts for  getting things done quickly you may find  it ugly and awkward but system  administrators love it because it gets  the job done to get started install the  Pearl interpreter then create a file  ending in.pl it's a scripting language  so we start with a shebang and point it  to the interpreter's binary declare a  variable with a dollar sign then print  it to the standard output and the  variable can be easily interpolated into  another string variables can be scoped  locally with the my keyword or globally  to an entire package with the hour  keyword the dollar sign creates a scalar  variable which is a single value like a  string or number if we change the dollar  sign to the at symbol this variable  becomes an array the index of any item  in the array can be accessed with  brackets now name the variable with a  percent sign to get a hash or key value  pair to access one of its Keys use  braces one of the design principles of  pearl is things that are different  should look different handle conditional  logic using IF or unless then make it  more concise by refactoring it to one  line then make it even more concise  using the ternary operator one of the  most unusual things about Pearl is that  your functions are defined as  subroutines instead of defining  parameters within parentheses like most  other languages any arguments passed to  the function can be accessed with the at  underscore array when it comes to  parsing text regex is built in and The  Binding operator can easily match a  string to a regular expression Pearl  also has a lot of magic features like  Auto vivification where if we try to  increment a value in an array that  doesn't exist it will automatically  bring to life all the elements in  between now to render code open the  terminal and run The Interpreter this  has been Pearl in 100 seconds hit the  like button and subscribe if you want to  see more short videos like this thanks  for watching and I will see you in the  next one
 

